<h2>Why hotels dominate the bill</h2>

<p>Hotels accounted for about 76% of the cost of the accommodation contracts, £1.3 billion of an
estimated £1.7 billion in the first seven months of 2024-25, and housed about 35% of the people in
asylum accommodation (National Audit Office, HC 874, paragraphs 1.18 and 1.14). The report puts
those two percentages in one sentence, and they are different kinds of measure: the 76% is a share
of spending across the first seven months of that financial year, while the 35% is a headcount on
a single day, 1 January 2025, after that window had closed. Hotels are disproportionately expensive, which
is what the pair is here to show, and the nightly rate below is the direct evidence for it. The two
percentages themselves are not on the same basis.</p>

